  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There continues to be an elevated risk for severe \nthunderstorms and flash flooding this evening and Wednesday, \nwith the greater risk through this evening.\n\nAs of early this evening, there are still strong to severe\nstorms ongoing across portions of Delmarva and southern NJ.\nFarther north and northwest over eastern PA northern NJ storms\nhave exited and the threat for additional severe weather has\ndiminished. For this reason, we cancelled the Tornado Watch for\nthese areas while farther south the watches remain in effect. \n\nStorm evolution will remain chaotic this evening with \nadditional storms likely over Delmarva and southern NJ. There \nwill continue to be a threat for severe weather, including \ntornados, along with flash flooding. The threat of severe \nweather should start to diminish by the second half of the \nevening while the threat for flash flooding could linger into \nthe overnight before the cold front arrives from the west and \npushes all the convection towards the coast by around 2 AM or \nso. During the rest of the overnight hours, convection may \npersist mainly across the southern Delmarva as the frontal \nboundary stalls across the region. The flash flood guidance for \nthis region is not as sensitive to excessive rainfall, but will \nstill bear monitoring through the early morning hours. \n\nOn Wednesday, the cold front will sag farther south and east and \ndepending on how quickly this clears our area there will be some \nshowers and thunderstorms, especially as additional shortwaves move \nthrough the flow within the upper-level trough overhead. This looks \nto be more concentrated across southern New Jersey to Delmarva but \ncould extend into the I-95 corridor for a time. There is some severe \nweather risk mainly near and south/east of the I-95 corridor with \nlocally damaging wind gusts the main threat. The greater instability \nand shear looks to reside across Delmarva on southward, where a \nlittle higher severe thunderstorm risk is indicated. Some locally \nexcessive rainfall is possible with some local flash flood risk, \nhowever this looks to be less compared to today given the shift \nfarther south and more into the coastal plain. Once the front moves \naway by later Wednesday, it turns drier for Thursday/Friday and \nright into the weekend.",
